<!-- WinSW service wrapper for MyIotSan (Windows).

  The Windows installer (myiotsan-setup-*.exe) already registers a service for you —
  this file is only for the portable .zip.

  A Go console app isn't a native Windows service, so wrap it with WinSW (or NSSM).

  WinSW (recommended):
    1. Download WinSW (https://github.com/winsw/winsw), put WinSW.exe next to the app
       and rename it myiotsan-service.exe.
    2. Put this file next to it as myiotsan-service.xml (same base name).
    3. Edit the <executable> / <workingdirectory> paths below.
    4. Run (elevated):  myiotsan-service.exe install   then   myiotsan-service.exe start
       Logs land next to the exe; manage via services.msc.

  NSSM alternative:
    nssm install MyIotSan "C:\myiotsan\myiotsan.exe"
    nssm set MyIotSan AppDirectory "C:\myiotsan"
    nssm set MyIotSan AppEnvironmentExtra KOPIV2_SUPERVISED=1
    nssm set MyIotSan AppExit Default Restart
    nssm start MyIotSan

  The app opens an MQTT broker on TCP 1883 for devices — add a Windows Firewall inbound
  rule for it, or no device will connect:
    netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="MyIotSan MQTT" dir=in action=allow protocol=TCP localport=1883

  onfailure restart + KOPIV2_SUPERVISED=1 means an in-app restart exits cleanly and the
  service control manager relaunches it.
-->
<service>
  <id>myiotsan</id>
  <name>MyIotSan IoT device hub</name>
  <description>MyIotSan — on-prem IoT device hub with an embedded MQTT broker.</description>
  <executable>C:\myiotsan\myiotsan.exe</executable>
  <workingdirectory>C:\myiotsan</workingdirectory>
  <env name="KOPIV2_SUPERVISED" value="1"/>
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="2 sec"/>
  <log mode="roll-by-size">
    <sizeThreshold>10240</sizeThreshold>
    <keepFiles>8</keepFiles>
  </log>
</service>
